            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.466,
            0.723,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Find the explicit fractions in case they are rational."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.494,
            0.885,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. The number 0.9 (which means 0.99999 … ) is a rational number. Using algebra (let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\underline{x} \\cong 0.\\overline{9},"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " multiply by 10, and subtract), explain why 0.9 is exactly equal to 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.57,
            0.885,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*5. We have seen that the repeating block of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 7 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a cyclic number. Try to find more numbers (n) whose reciprocals "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\left( { \\frac { 1 } { n } } \\right)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " produce decimals with repeating blocks that are cyclic."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.657,
            0.885,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Non-uniqueness of decimal representations.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Just as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 = { \\frac { 1 0 } { 1 0 } } = { \\frac { 1 0 0 } { 1 0 0 } } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " rational numbers can have two decimal forms. Any terminating decimal has an alternative with repeating 9s: 1.000 … = 0.999 …, 2.47000 … = 2.46999 …."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.754,
            0.886,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is it not surprising that 0.999 … = 1? Many would have guessed that it is slightly less than 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
